{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>And the sun rose for him.</b> Every single day from the tekufah of Tevet until the tekufah of Tammuz, the sun advances and hastens its rising over the previous day by close to 36 parts, and likewise it delays and holds back its setting from the previous day by close to 36 parts; in all, 72 parts — every day is longer than the previous day. And the asmakhta is from here: <b>and it rose for him</b> — לו in gematria comes to this. How is it known to us? The sun does not travel on the day of the tekufah of Tevet but only along the southern wind, and from there on the day grows longer and the sun advances its rising from within the east and delays its setting within the west, until it rises at the midpoint of the east and sets at the midpoint of the west on the day of the tekufah of Nisan, when the days and nights are equal, and it travels along two winds in twelve hours. It turns out that the day of the tekufah of Nisan is longer than the day of the tekufah of Tevet by six hours. If so, we find for one tekufah, which is a quarter of the year, the time increases by a quarter day, which is six hours. And for a solar month, which is thirty days and ten and a half hours, the time increases by two hours. And for half a month, one hour. For a quarter of the month, which is seven days and fourteen and a half hours and 135 parts, the time increases by 540 parts, which are half an hour. For twenty-six hours and nineteen parts, which are one-seventh of a quarter of the month, the time increases by one-seventh of 540 parts, which is 77 parts. And corresponding to the surplus you have over a day — close to one-thirteenth of the seventh of a quarter of the month, which is two hours and nineteen parts — correspondingly you must subtract close to one-thirteenth of the 77 parts, and 72 parts remain. For thus the day increases over its fellow every day from the tekufah of Tevet until the tekufah of Tammuz, and likewise it decreases from its fellow by 72 parts every day from the tekufah of Tammuz until the tekufah of Tevet." "<b>And he limped upon his thigh.</b> He did not manage to pass Penuel before the sun rose. Another interpretation: now that the sun rose, it became apparent that he was limping upon his thigh — like ״and it was morning, and behold she was Leah,״ for until now it was not known that she was Leah."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "The technical calendrical arithmetic is translated literally; the numerical values (36 parts, 72 parts, etc.) follow the Hebrew text exactly. Some phrases are textually dense but not corrupt; all figures preserved as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:07:16.536487391Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}